# config/hooks/fragments/ — the hook extension point
#
# Established by ticket 009. This file exists to keep the directory in git and
# to carry the convention; the provisioner ignores dotfiles, so nothing here
# reaches a settings file.
#
# WHY THIS DIRECTORY EXISTS
#
# Tickets 020 and 027 (and anything later) need their own hook entries. Without
# this directory they would each edit config/hooks/base.settings.json, which
# breaks one-file-one-ticket and makes two tickets unable to run in parallel.
# Instead: drop a file in here. `base.settings.json` is never edited again.
#
# HOW TO ADD A HOOK
#
# 1. Create `config/hooks/fragments/<your-ticket-subject>.json`. Name it after
#    what it does, not after your ticket number — the filename is what an
#    operator reads when a hook misbehaves. Sorted filename order is the merge
#    order, and it is stable, so a fragment that must run before another can be
#    named to say so.
#
# 2. Put a settings-shaped object in it. Only `hooks` and `permissions.allow`
#    are merged; anything else is refused loudly rather than silently dropped,
#    so a fragment cannot quietly change a permission mode, a model, or the
#    environment of every agent in the office.
#
#        {
#          "hooks": {
#            "PreToolUse": [
#              {
#                "matcher": "Edit|Write|NotebookEdit",
#                "hooks": [
#                  {
#                    "type": "command",
#                    "command": "${REPO_ROOT}/hooks/pretooluse-guard.sh",
#                    "timeout": 2
#                  }
#                ]
#              }
#            ]
#          }
#        }
#
# 3. Re-run `scripts/provision-user.sh <user>` for every account, and RESTART
#    every running agent. Hook config is read once at launch — there is no hot
#    reload (spike 001 §8.2). A re-provisioned hook does not reach a live
#    session, and the session will keep looking healthy while it ignores you.
#
# MERGE RULES (asserted by provision.test.ts)
#
# - Arrays CONCATENATE, they never replace. base.settings.json's entries come
#   first, then each fragment in sorted filename order. Two fragments
#   registering for the same event both survive, and both of their hooks run —
#   Claude Code runs the hooks of one event group concurrently, so adding one
#   costs its own duration, not the sum.
# - An entry that is byte-identical to one already merged is dropped, so
#   re-provisioning is idempotent and a fragment duplicating a base entry
#   cannot double-post an event.
# - `permissions.allow` entries are unioned, de-duplicated, and sorted.
#
# PLACEHOLDERS
#
# These are substituted at provision time. An unknown `${...}` is a hard error:
# a placeholder that silently rendered empty would produce a hook that posts to
# the wrong place, or a guard script path of "/hooks/guard.sh".
#
#   ${EVENT_SERVER_ORIGIN}  https origin of the event server, no trailing slash
#   ${HOOK_INGEST_TOKEN}    this account's ingest token (goes in the query string)
#   ${CA_CERT_PATH}         the certificate agents trust, i.e. NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS
#   ${REPO_ROOT}            this repo's checkout on the host
#   ${USER_HOME}            the target account's home directory
#   ${TARGET_USER}          the target account's name
#
# TWO THINGS A FRAGMENT CANNOT DO
#
# - `SessionStart` must stay a `command` hook. HTTP hooks are never dispatched
#   for it — reproduced five times in spike 001 §7, and the installed build says
#   so in as many words ("HTTP hooks are not supported for SessionStart"). The
#   base template owns that entry; a fragment adding an `http` SessionStart hook
#   would be accepted by the merge and then never fire.
# - A `command` hook inherits the agent's launch environment, which carries
#   $NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS, $OFFICE_EVENT_SERVER_ORIGIN and
#   $OFFICE_HOOK_INGEST_TOKEN. It does NOT get a shell profile: tmux runs the
#   command through a non-interactive shell, so anything from ~/.bashrc or
#   ~/.local/bin is not on PATH. Name binaries by absolute path.
